From 6832bf0ac0941c91a7934731795d78206326e2c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:00:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] game panes: a front plane, shader parameters, and room for a
 real game

Three things a whole game needs that a demo did not.

A FRONT PLANE: a second indexed plane composited after the sprites, out of
the global palette. Text drawn into the world goes under them, which is
right for a scene and wrong for a score -- an alien flying across it
should pass behind. SetPlane() picks which plane the drawing calls write
to, so nothing else in the API doubles.

SHADER PARAMETERS: sixteen floats in the pane's own allocation that
either shader reads as p.param(i). A shader with no way to be told
anything can only ever draw one thing; with this, twelve backdrops are
one compile and a number, not twelve compiles.

And room: 128 sprites a frame and 128 shapes rather than 64 -- a
formation of forty, six mines, a boss, four pilots, a saucer and a ship
is 53 on screen and 43 shapes before anything unusual happens. Four font
slots, so a title size and a score size do not mean asking for the font
twice a frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
